# The test character: a skeleton with no art on it.
#
# Every bone here exists to exercise one thing the rig has to do. It is drawn
# by RigDraw's debug mode -- one colour per bone -- so the system can be built
# and checked long before a character is drawn to hang on it.
#
# ── THE ANGLE CONVENTION, because getting it wrong is invisible in the file ──
#
# Screen space: 0 = right, +90 = DOWN, -90 = up. Angles are LOCAL to the
# parent, so a bone's world angle is its parent's plus its own.
#
# The chest sits at -90 (straight up). A CLAVICLE runs out sideways from the
# top of it, and the arm hangs off the clavicle -- so arm angles are measured
# from the shoulder, not from the spine:
#
#     arm hanging straight down .... local  90 from a sideways clavicle
#     arm straight out ............. local   0
#     arm raised above the shoulder  local -90
#
# ── WHY SHOULDERS AND HIPS ARE BONES ──
#
# Arms used to hang off the chest directly, which meant a shrug could only
# rotate the upper arm: the shoulder itself could not rise, and a shrug that
# does not lift the shoulders is not a shrug. The clavicle also gives the coat
# somewhere honest to hang from.
#
# The pelvis is the same argument from the other end. Sway rotates the hips,
# and with no hip bones there was nothing between the spine and the ground for
# it to rotate -- the whole body pivoted about a point instead of the weight
# moving from one leg to the other.

# ── WHAT `facing` IS NUMERICALLY, because -0.3 does not mean what it looks like ──
#
# facing is the COSINE of how far the hand has rolled about its own length, not
# a fraction of "turned-ness". So:
#
#     +-1.0   palm (or back) square to the viewer -- full width
#     +-0.7   rolled 45 degrees
#     +-0.3   rolled 73 degrees -- very nearly EDGE-ON
#      0.0    edge-on: no width at all, only the hand's thickness
#
# The first draft of these poses read -0.3 as "backs of the hands, mostly" and
# got a hand seen almost end-on: a sliver, with the fingers curling sideways out
# of the palm plane into a claw. A hand hanging at somebody's side shows the
# BACK of the hand to a viewer in front of them, which is -0.85, not -0.3.

# ── Spine ──
bone root    len 0   angle 0
bone hips    parent root  len 0   angle 0    sway 1.0
bone chest   parent hips  len 46  angle -90  breath 1.0  sway 0.5
bone neck    parent chest len 12  angle 0    breath 0.4  gaze 0.3
head head    parent neck  len 20  angle 0    size 40     gaze 1.0  breath 0.2

# ── Pelvis, and enough leg to see it working. The box crops the legs; they are
#    here so sway has something to move and the stance reads as weight. ──
bone hipL    parent hips len 13 angle 180  sway 0.6
bone hipR    parent hips len 13 angle 0    sway 0.6
bone legL    parent hipL len 40 angle -90
bone legR    parent hipR len 40 angle 90

# ── Shoulders. A clavicle out from the top of the spine, then the arm. ──
bone clavL   parent chest len 16 angle 90   breath 0.6
bone armL.up parent clavL len 34 angle 90   breath 0.2
bone armL.fore parent armL.up len 30 angle 10
hand handL   parent armL.fore len 0 angle 0 atlas hands size 22

bone clavR   parent chest len 16 angle -90  breath 0.6
bone armR.up parent clavR len 34 angle -90  breath 0.2
bone armR.fore parent armR.up len 30 angle -10
hand handR   parent armR.fore len 0 angle 0 atlas hands size 22

# ── Cloth: a coat hanging off the clavicles, so it moves when the shoulders do ──
cloth coatL parent clavL segs 5 len 13 stiff 0.62 damp 0.93 gravity 430 drag 1.2
cloth coatR parent clavR segs 5 len 13 stiff 0.62 damp 0.93 gravity 430 drag 1.2

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Poses. The ease is inoutquad throughout: a limb accelerates out of rest and
# decelerates into place. "outquad" and "outback" leave at full speed, which on
# a big arm swing puts a sixth of the travel in the first frame and reads as a
# snap -- keep them for a gesture that is meant to be sharp.
#
# A pose says only what it changes; every bone it does not name goes back to
# rest, so poses cannot silently compose.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Arms down, a little away from the body, shoulders level.
pose idle 0.5s inoutquad
  chest      -90
  clavL      90
  clavR      -90
  armL.up    80
  armL.fore  10
  armR.up    -80
  armR.fore  -10
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      relaxed facing -0.85

# Shoulders UP -- which is the half a shrug was missing -- forearms out, palms
# turned to the viewer. The hands travel from backs-out to palms-out, which is
# the transition the facing scalar exists for.
# A shrug is a MOVEMENT, not a posture, which is why it is the pose with
# keyframes on it. The shoulders go up fast, are seen up, and come back down;
# arriving somewhere and staying there is a person standing oddly.
#
# The arms go fully out to the sides -- upper arms out and a little down,
# forearms carrying on outward and a little up, palms turned over. The snap up
# is outback on purpose: the note above warns that "out" easings leave at full
# speed, and that is exactly what a shrug wants and what an arm swing does not.
pose shrug 0.22s outback hold 0.45s then idle
  chest      -86
  neck       6
  clavL      54
  clavR      -54
  armL.up    52
  armL.fore  -18
  armR.up    -52
  armR.fore  18
  handL      open facing 0.9
  handR      open facing 0.9

# ...and down again. A keyframe inherits everything it does not name, so the
# arms stay out while only the shoulders drop.
# The arms stay out for a beat before `then idle` drops them: a shrug that
# snaps straight back has not been seen.
key 0.5s inoutquad hold 1.1s
  clavL      72
  clavR      -72
  armL.up    38
  armL.fore  -18
  armR.up    -38
  armR.fore  18

# Right arm out toward the map, left arm at rest.
pose point 0.4s inoutquad
  chest      -92
  neck       -8
  clavL      90
  clavR      -84
  armL.up    80
  armL.fore  10
  armR.up    -12
  armR.fore  -8
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      point facing -0.85
  head       -4

# Both arms raised, palms fully to the viewer. The ABSOLUTE CINEMA pose.
#
# THE ELBOWS BREAK OUTWARD, not inward. Raising your arms with the palms turned
# to somebody rotates the whole arm at the shoulder, and the elbow goes with it
# -- so the forearm angles are the opposite sign to the upper arms', and the
# hand turning about its own axis (facing runs to +1) is the end of a movement
# the elbow is already making. With the elbows bent the same way as the upper
# arms the arm is nearly straight, the rotation has nowhere to show, and the
# hands look stuck on.
pose hands_up 0.55s inoutquad
  chest      -90
  neck       -4
  clavL      74
  clavR      -74
  armL.up    -46
  armL.fore  -36
  armR.up    46
  armR.fore  36
  handL      open facing 1.0
  handR      open facing 1.0

# Right hand up to the chin, head tilted onto it.
pose think 0.6s inoutquad
  chest      -88
  neck       10
  head       8
  clavL      90
  clavR      -80
  armL.up    80
  armL.fore  10
  armR.up    -150
  armR.fore  -133
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      fist facing -0.6
